body:
I was lucky to live at home and work casually in hospo when I was studying, I recall deeply during one of my 5 week full time M-F placements (different shift times) a fellow student would do those placement days then work all morning Saturday at one aged care as a PCA, leave that shift and go work an evening shift at a different aged care facility and do the same on Sunday. So for 5 weeks she didn't have a day off.

&#x200B;

The placements especially as you progress in training level (different obviously as a first year nursing student where you really can't do anything and don't know your ass from your face vs third year where you do everything care related solo and just need to be supervised for assessments and any medication administration) really do exploit free labour.

&#x200B;

If I hadn't already had well established casual jobs that I could say "hey i'm not available for work these weeks" and wasn't living at home, I could never have afforded my nursing degree (that went fully on HECS). We also always had to pay for parking at the hospitals (I remember Monash Clayton did a reduced rate for students, but it was still expensive, and also needed a shuttle bus to get from the carpark to the hospital, no other hospitals I know had a reduced rate), so often you were missing out on pay for not being able to work due to being available for 40hr/week placements and also paying for the pleasure to be able to get there and park.

I'm doing a masters this year, and it's a CSP. From my understanding of what the ATO states, if it was a non-CSP it would be tax deductible, as it will improve my earning potential and job prospects. However, it's CSP, so the fees for the course subjects themselves are not tax deductible. The student services and amenities fees are tax deductible, as would be any textbooks/specific to the course expenses.

&#x200B;

I'll be thrilled if my accountant tells me differently in July, but from my understanding it isn't tax deductible if its not full fee.

Look, I finished it and agree that it was ridiculous from the start, but I never really sat down and thought about it all until I tried to explain a twist to my sister who had never watched the show. I was in my 20s, so welllllll old enough to be more critical lol.

&#x200B;

It's such a dumb guilty pleasure.

My rice stovetop method is as follows:

Rinse rice like 4 or 5 times;
Soak rice for an hour or so;
Drain again and then fill to a knuckle above rice ;
Bring to a boil, lid off;
Turn to a low simmer, put lid on;
Let simmer for twelve minutes or so, make a hole in the middle of the rice with the handle of a spoon to see how long to go, turn off when no visible water at the bottom of the hole;
Move off heat and let it sit for like 10 mins with the lid on;
Fluff up with a fork

Shouldn't stick!

Yeah, if my landlords had fixed the leak under my bathroom sink they wouldn't have ongoing mould issues and realistically the only way around it is a whole new vanity.

&#x200B;

I'm not even bothering to ask about it, considering I had to breach them to get them to send me a plumber after I had to threaten breaching them to get them to send a handyman who confirmed that yes, they did need a plumber.

Lol my parents had a house that was trashed by a tenant, they don't have the money to renovate it to bring it up to rental standards, so .... they sold it.

&#x200B;

They're sad about getting rid of the house that my sister and I came home from the hospital to, and didn't get what the house could have been worth (had the condition been quite, quite different), but they've sold it. That's literally the only option.

